Med uses `ElMessage` imported directly (not `this.$message`) → my $message stub doesn't intercept, and real ElMessage needs `document`. Fix: shim `globalThis.document` minimally? Better: the harness should stub the ElMessage module... can't easily.

Cheapest legit fix: give the harness a tiny document stub so ElMessage can be constructed (it appends DOM nodes...). Real ElMessage will try to create elements → needs a DOM. Risky rabbit hole.

Alternative: check how test_nr_view handled it — NR view uses a `notify()` method (that's why I built it). Med uses ElMessage directly. For a Node harness, add a minimal `globalThis.document = { body: { appendChild(){}, removeChild(){} }, createElement: () => ({ style:{}, setAttribute(){}, addEventListener(){}, appendChild(){}, remove(){}, classList:{add(){},remove(){}} }), ... }` — ElMessage also uses Vue render → needs more (createApp with container). Too deep.

Better approach: **stub the ElMessage import at the module level** by intercepting via vite ssr transform? I can pass `resolve.alias` in the harness's createServer config to map 'element-plus' to a local shim file exporting `{ ElMessage: {...}, ElLoading: {...} , default: {}}`. But med imports `{ ElMessage, ElLoading } from 'element-plus'` and also components from the template (SSR render isn't happening here, only method execution → template not compiled/used? ssrLoadModule compiles the SFC including imports of element-plus? The view's script imports only ElMessage/ElLoading presumably; components are global via app.use). So aliasing 'element-plus' to a tiny shim works for method testing!

But careful: ssrLoadModule of the SFC may import other things from element-plus (icons?) — shim must export * from real element-plus but override ElMessage? Simplest: shim file re-exports everything from the real module but replaces ElMessage/ElLoading with stubs:

```js
// /tmp/ep_shim.mjs  (put in r-scripts/tools/_ep_shim.mjs, temp)
import * as real from 'element-plus';
export const ElMessage = { info:()=>{}, success:()=>{}, error:()=>{}, warning:()=>{}, closeAll:()=>{} };
export const ElLoading = { service: () => ({ close(){} }) };
export default { ...real, ElMessage, ElLoading };
export * from 'element-plus';  // conflicts with named above → must omit duplicates
```
`export *` skips names explicitly exported locally? Per spec, explicit exports take precedence over `export *` ✓ (ambiguity rules: local export wins).

Then alias in createServer: `resolve: { alias: { 'element-plus': path to shim } }`. The shim's own `import * as real from 'element-plus'` would then resolve to itself (infinite). Use absolute real path: import from '.../node_modules/element-plus/es/index.mjs'? Fragile.

Simpler: shim doesn't re-export real at all — just provides ElMessage/ElLoading stubs. If the SFC script imports other EP names (e.g. `ElMessageBox`), they'd be undefined → only matters if called in the tested paths. Let me check what med imports from element-plus.

Actually even simpler: make the stub a Proxy that returns a no-op function/object for ANY named import ✓ robust:

```js
const noop = () => {};
const factory = () => new Proxy(function (...a) { return factory(); }, { get: () => factory(), apply: (t, _, a) => factory() });
export default factory();
export const ElMessage = ...
```
Hmm ESM named imports need real bindings. Use:
```js
const stub = new Proxy({}, { get: () => () => ({ close(){} }) });
export const ElMessage = new Proxy({}, { get: () => (o) => vmNotices.push(...) });
export const ElLoading = { service: () => ({ close() {} }) };
```
and to survive unknown named imports, vite alias module can use `export` with a catch-all? Not possible in ESM. So I need the exact list of EP imports in med's script. Let me grep the import lines of PERMANOVA_Med.vue.

Given budget (~20k left), I must be pragmatic. Alternative approach that avoids all this: make the harness not call the view's import applier at all when ElMessage is hit... no, the whole point is end state.

Wait — simpler! Patch `globalThis.document` is needed by EP's message. But there's an even easier trick: the view calls ElMessage at the END of confirmImport (line 1226/1229 = success/error messages). The state assignment (Str fields) happens BEFORE those calls, and the throw propagates out of confirmImport — caught by its try/catch → which calls ElMessage.error → throws again → propagates to driveImport → uncaught. So: wrap the applier call in try/catch in driveImport! Then state assertions still hold (they're set before the message). Slight ugliness but pragmatic and zero fragility. The test then documents "the view shouts via ElMessage which needs a DOM" — acceptable for a methods harness, as long as assertions target state.

Do that: `try { applier.call(vm); } catch (e) { /* ElMessage 需要 DOM */ }`. Also handleFileChange may call ElMessage in old style (inside reader.onload async → unhandled rejection, printed but harmless).

Also, for notices-based assertions I'll skip them (med uses ElMessage; after refactor I'll route user-visible errors into `importError` state which IS assertable ✓).

Let me patch the harness and rerun.

